</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Benchmarks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.3cm;">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defects4J.</span>
To evaluate the repair effectiveness, we use the widely studied benchmark Defects4J <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Just et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. Similar to prior APR studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we separate Defects4J into V1.2 and V2.0. Defects4J V1.2 consists of 391 bugs (removed 4 deprecated bugs), and Defects4J V2.0 introduces 438 new bugs. In this work, we follow prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and categorize Defects4J into three repair scenarios, including single-function (SF), single-hunk (SH), and single-line (SL) bugs. Note that single-hunk is a subset of single-function and single-line is a subset of single-hunk bugs.
In addition, we will also implement fixes on the multi-function (MF) scenario.
The statistic of each scenario is presented in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1. Research Questions ‣ 4. Experiment Setup ‣ Repair Ingredients Are All You Need: Improving Large Language Model-Based Program Repair via Repair Ingredients Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RWB.</span>
To evaluate the generalizability study, we use the recent repair benchmark RWB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Note that the pre-training data for ChatGPT (GPT-3.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2021a</a>)</cite>) was collected before September 2021 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2022</a>)</cite>, and the pre-training data for DeepSeek-Coder was collected from GitHub before February 2023 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI, 2023</a>)</cite>. To assess data leakage of ChatGPT and DeepSeek, Yin <span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> collected two datasets to assess data leakage. The first dataset (RWB V1.0) comprises bug-fixing commits after October 2021, while the second dataset (RWB V2.0) includes bug-fixing commits after March 2023, resulting 44 and 29 single-function bugs, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We considered recent APR works to serve as baselines. This includes:
ChatRepair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2024</a>)</cite>, ThinkRepair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, RepairAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bouzenia et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, FitRepair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, GAMMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, TENURE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Tare <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, AlphaRepair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2022</a>)</cite>, RAP-Gen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, KNOD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, Recoder-T <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, TBar <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. Following the common practice in the APR community <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, we reuse the reported results from previous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> instead of directly running the APR tools.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Vector Database</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct a vector database for the external ingredients search, we utilize the TRANSFER dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022</a>)</cite> as the retrieval corpus and the text-embedding-3-large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite> as the embedding model.
TRANSFER dataset contains about one million BFPs and corresponding fix templates that have been used to drive the template-based APR works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
Considering the cost of embedding, we randomly selected 100K samples from TRANSFER for the vector database construction. We use the exact match strategy to filter out overlapping samples with those in benchmarks to avoid data leakage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Implementation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReinFix</span> is built on top of the LangChain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">LangChain, 2024</a>)</cite> framework, where we use gpt-3.5-turbo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2021a</a>)</cite> and gpt-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2023b</a>)</cite> as base models, and set a sampling temperature of 1.
In fault localization (FL), to avoid additional biases introduced by the FL tool, we follow recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> under conditions of perfect fault localization.
When generating patches, we limit the number of repair attempts to a maximum of 3 per bug. For each attempt, the maximum number of repair suggestions is set to 3, and for each repair suggestion, up to 5 candidate patches are considered. Therefore, the total maximum patch space size is 3*3*5=45.
For validation, only the top-1 plausible patch that passes all test cases is retained.
Finally, the plausible patches are manually reviewed to verify the semantic correctness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Evaluation</h2>

